{"id":"health","corpus":"aws","title":"AWS Health","sourceUrl":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/health/latest/ug/what-is-aws-health.html","license":"AWS Documentation © Amazon Web Services, Inc.","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"AWS Health"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Health provides ongoing visibility into your resource performance and the availability of your AWS services and accounts. You can use AWS Health events to learn how service and resource changes might affect your applications running on AWS. AWS Health provides relevant and timely information to help you manage events in progress. AWS Health also helps you be aware of and to prepare for planned activities. The service delivers alerts and notifications triggered by changes in the health of AWS resources, so that you get near-instant event visibility and guidance to help accelerate troubleshooting."},{"kind":"p","text":"All customers can use the AWS Health Dashboard, powered by the AWS Health API. The dashboard requires no setup, and it's ready to use for authenticated AWS users. For more service highlights, see the AWS Health Dashboard detail page."},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Health provides a console, called the AWS Health Dashboard, to all customers. You do not need to write code or perform any actions to set up the dashboard."},{"kind":"p","text":"To learn the basics about AWS Health and terms that you will encounter while you use the service, To understand the basics of AWS Health see Concepts for AWS Health."},{"kind":"li","text":"The AWS Health Dashboard is available for all AWS customers at no additional cost."},{"kind":"li","text":"All AWS customers can receive AWS Health events through Amazon EventBridge at no additional cost."},{"kind":"li","text":"If you have an AWS Business Support+, AWS Enterprise Support, or AWS Unified Operations plan, you can use the AWS Health API to integrate with in-house and third-party systems. If you're in an AWS Region that doesn't offer one of these AWS Support plans, or if you haven't transitioned to one of these plans, you can use the AWS Health API with a Business, Enterprise On-Ramp, or Enterprise Support plan. For more information, see the AWS Health API Reference."},{"kind":"li","text":"For more information about available AWS Support plans, see AWS Support."}],"lemmas":{"provides":["provides","provide"],"provided":["provided","provide"],"providing":["providing","provide"],"ongoings":["ongoings","ongoing"],"visibilities":["visibility","visibilities"],"performances":["performance","performances"],"availabilities":["availability","availabilities"],"services":["service","services"],"serviced":["serviced","service"],"servicing":["servicing","service"],"accounts":["account","accounts"],"accounting":["accounting","account"],"accounted":["accounted","account"],"uses":["use","uses"],"using":["using","use"],"used":["used","use"],"events":["event","events"],"learns":["learns","learn"],"learning":["learning","learn"],"learned":["learned","learn"],"changes":["change","changes"],"changing":["changing","change"],"changed":["changed","change"],"affects":["affect","affects"],"affecting":["affecting","affect"],"affected":["affected","affect"],"applications":["application","applications"],"running":["running","run"],"runnings":["running","runnings"],"runs":["run","runs"],"ran":["ran","run"],"relevants":["relevants","relevant"],"timelies":["timelies","timely"],"helping":["helping","help"],"helped":["helped","help"],"manages":["manages","manage"],"managing":["managing","manage"],"managed":["managed","manage"],"progresses":["progress","progresses"],"progressing":["progressing","progress"],"progressed":["progressed","progress"],"helps":["help","helps"],"awares":["awares","aware"],"prepares":["prepares","prepare"],"prepared":["prepared","prepare"],"preparing":["preparing","prepare"],"planned":["planned","plan"],"planneds":["planneds","planned"],"planning":["planning","plan"],"planed":["planed","plan"],"activities":["activity","activities"],"delivers":[